WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.888
This guided practice checks whether you understand
the three common COUNT patterns

00:00:04.775 --> 00:00:09.312
Before opening the answer explain the difference in
your own words COUNT

00:00:09.338 --> 00:00:13.688
star counts input rows If a row exists in the query
result

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:17.550
before aggregation it contributes to COUNT star
regardless of whether some of

00:00:17.562 --> 00:00:21.800
its columns are NULL COUNT of a column counts only
non-NULL values

00:00:21.825 --> 00:00:25.262
in that column This makes it useful when the
presence of a

00:00:25.288 --> 00:00:29.375
value has meaning such as counting admissions with a
recorded discharge time

00:00:30.262 --> 00:00:35.675
COUNT DISTINCT column counts unique non-NULL values
For example if one patient

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:41.312
has several admissions COUNT DISTINCT subject_id
counts that patient once The

00:00:41.325 --> 00:00:46.150
best way to remember these patterns is not by
memorizing definitions Ask

00:00:46.175 --> 00:00:51.162
what you want to count rows recorded values or
unique values Try

00:00:51.175 --> 00:00:55.138
giving your own example for each form If you can
explain why

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:58.412
the three answers might be different on the same
table you understand

00:00:58.425 --> 00:01:00.738
the idea well enough to use it in a real query
